  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Multiple rounds of showers and scattered \nthunderstorms are expected through Wednesday, with localized heavy \nrainfall/flooding possible into tonight.\n\nLatest surface analysis shows a broad surface low and cold front \nextending from southern Ontario (just east of Toronto) southwestward \nto eastern Ohio/northwestern PA. This system will slowly wobble its \nway eastward this afternoon...and should be aligned along a rough \nupper Saint Lawrence Valley to central New York line by early this \nevening. Out ahead of the low and front fairly widespread showers \nand scattered thunderstorms will continue and/or redevelop \ntoday...with the very moist environment (PWATs of 1.5 to 1.75 \ninches) supporting a continued potential for localized heavy \nrainfall and flash flooding. Meanwhile on the west side of this \nsystem convective coverage/potential will both tend to be lower \n(particularly across the Niagara Frontier)...however diurnal warming \nof a still-moist airmass should still lead to the redevelopment of \nsome additional showers and thunderstorms this afternoon. \n\nAs the surface low/front drifts across eastern New York tonight, \nwraparound moisture will support continued chances for showers \nacross the entirety of the forecast area...though lingering \npotential for heavy rainfall/flooding across our eastern zones \nshould diminish following the passage of the low axis/cold front and \nthe departure of the deepest moisture. \n\nKey Message 2...Chances for showers and thunderstorms are expected \nto linger into Thursday, with drier conditions for the end of the \nwork week.\n\nAn anomalous strong area of surface low pressure will gradually \nslide east off the coast of southern New England Thursday. This will \nsupport the plume of moisture to pull east, however some diurnally \ndriven convection will be possible for areas inland from the lakes. \nOtherwise, expect cooler temperatures with highs in the 70s.\n\nHigh pressure will then move in across the eastern Great Lakes for \nfor the end of the work week supporting dry and seasonal weather.",
